## 3. Data flow

Where an envelope is created, mutated, validated, and read — and which profile applies at each
hop. The two ✅ gates are **mandatory**; they are the whole enforcement surface for INV-1.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    A["PAEOS goal seals<br/>L15/L16"] --> B["I-OUTBOX<br/>runtime/emission/outbox.py<br/>(no sakg import)"]
    B --> C["Emission Bundle<br/>F-BUNDLE, append-only outbox"]
    C -->|"pull, never push"| D{"✅ GATE 1<br/>validate vs<br/>#ingest-envelope"}
    D -->|reject| E["ingest defect<br/>recorded, node dropped"]
    D -->|accept| F["I-INGEST stamps derived fields:<br/>confidence ← derivation policy<br/>ingested_at ← now<br/>status ← candidate (already pinned)"]
    F --> G{"✅ GATE 2<br/>validate vs ROOT<br/>(stored profile)"}
    G -->|accept| H["I-STORE append-only write<br/>namespace-partitioned"]
    H --> I["S3 Truth Maintenance<br/>confidence recompute<br/>→ re-validate vs ROOT"]
    I --> H
    H --> J["F-PROJ projection read<br/>node_summary = {id, label, summary}<br/>tier SUMMARY, trust untrusted"]
```

**Field lifecycle.** Which actor may write which field, and when:

| Field | Producer (ingest) | I-INGEST | I-STORE / S3 recompute | Ever mutated after write? |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `id`, `type`, `namespace` | writes | — | — | never (identity) |
| `label`, `summary`, `facets`, `coordinates` | writes | — | — | never — a change is a **new node + `supersedes` edge** (D-7) |
| `provenance.*` | writes `origin`, `author_kind`, `source_hash` | writes `ingested_at` | — | never |
| `evidence` | writes (may be `[]`) | — | appends on evidence binding | append-only |
| `status` | pinned `candidate` (D-6) | — | transitions per §4 | yes, per §4 table only |
| `confidence` | **forbidden** (INV-1) | writes initial derived value | recomputes | yes, by recompute only |
| `valid_from` | writes | — | — | never |
| `valid_until` | writes (usually `null`) | — | closes on supersede/refute | `null → timestamp` only |
| `falsifier` | writes (may be `null`) | — | — | never |

**Data-flow completeness.** Every field in §2.2 has exactly one writer at each stage and at
least one reader (GATE 1/2, or F-PROJ's `node_summary`). No field is produced and never
consumed; no field is consumed and never produced. That is the "no gap" check for §3.

