## 4. State machine — the `status` lattice

The envelope schema pins the **state space** and the **per-state well-formedness invariant**.
The **transition relation** is not expressible in a single-document schema (a document shows
one state, never a pair) and is therefore frozen here as the normative input to `I-INGEST` and
`I-STORE`.

```mermaid
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> candidate : ingest (§6.1b — the ONLY entry point)
    candidate --> canonical : T1 · evidence binding (≥1 EvidenceRef)
    candidate --> refuted : T2 · falsifier observed
    candidate --> superseded : T3 · a newer node supersedes it
    canonical --> superseded : T4
    canonical --> refuted : T5 · falsifier observed
    superseded --> refuted : T6 · later shown false
    refuted --> [*] : terminal — RETAINED, never deleted
```

**Legal transitions.**

| # | From → To | Precondition | Effect on fields |
|---|---|---|---|
| T1 | `candidate → canonical` | ≥1 `EvidenceRef` bound. **Frequency counters are never a precondition** (×5, INV-2). | `evidence` non-empty; `confidence` recomputed |
| T2 | `candidate → refuted` | `falsifier` non-null **and observed** | `valid_until` ← observation time |
| T3 | `candidate → superseded` | an incoming `supersedes` edge exists | `valid_until` ← supersession time |
| T4 | `canonical → superseded` | as T3 | as T3 |
| T5 | `canonical → refuted` | as T2 | as T2 |
| T6 | `superseded → refuted` | as T2 | as T2 |

**Forbidden transitions** (I-STORE validator; each closes a named failure):

| Forbidden | Why |
|---|---|
| `* → candidate` | Demotion to candidate would erase an evidence binding. De-canonicalization is `refuted` or `superseded`, never a rewind. |
| `superseded → canonical`, `refuted → canonical` | Resurrection. A reversal is a **new node** that `supersedes` the old one (D-7, ✓4). |
| `refuted → *` | `refuted` is terminal at v1 — it is the retained, high-value record (FPL-2). |
| `* → deleted` | No such state exists (INV-4). Unrepresentable, not merely forbidden. |
| any transition into `canonical` justified by counters | ×5 in one line. Frequency raises **review priority** only (facet `sakg.core.review_priority`). |

**Per-state well-formedness** (this part *is* in the schema, as `allOf` of four `if/then`):

| State | Schema-enforced invariant | Rationale |
|---|---|---|
| `candidate` | no extra constraint; `evidence` may be `[]` | candidates are unproven by definition (✓6) |
| `canonical` | `evidence: {minItems: 1}` | INV-2 |
| `superseded` | `valid_until: {type: string}` (non-null) | INV-5 — a superseded node with an open window is incoherent |
| `refuted` | `falsifier: {type: string}` **and** `valid_until: {type: string}` | INV-3 — you can only be refuted against a stated falsifier |

Every state has an entry (ingest for `candidate`; T1–T6 for the rest) and an exit (`refuted` is
the intentional terminal, and its exit is *retention*, not deletion). No unreachable state, no
state without an invariant.

